※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ **A function answers in the allocator its signature names**: `→ T in a`, or `⎕heap` where it
※ names none.  An answer made anywhere else is copied there before the function leaves -- before
※ what it put off runs, so an arena given back on the way out has been copied out of first.  That
※ is how a value made in a scope's own arena reaches past the scope: as a copy, in an allocator
※ that lasts.
※
※ Text in the image is answered as it is, nothing ever giving it back.  And a value of the heap's
※ is the caller's alone, so only a temporary is answered as it is; a name holding one is copied.
※
※ Compare: **C++**, whose return of a container allocated with a local `pmr` resource is a copy
※ into the caller's (or a dangling one, if it is moved); **Rust**, which refuses to return what
※ borrows a local arena; **Zig**, where the caller passes the allocator and nothing checks it.

let std := ⎕import("std")

※ made in an arena of the call's own: copied into ⎕heap
fn local(first: str) → str:
    let scratch: mut arena = ⎕arena
    defer ⎕empty(scratch)
    let both: str = first ⧺ "<-" in scratch
    both

※ made in the other arena it was handed: copied into the one the signature names
fn other(a: &mut arena, b: &mut arena, v: u16) → str in a:
    ⍕v in b

※ handed over and saying nothing of where it was made: copied
fn kept(s: str) → str:
    s

※ text in the image: as it is
fn literal() → str:
    "lit"

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let x: mut arena = ⎕arena
    defer ⎕empty(x)
    let y: mut arena = ⎕arena
    let s: str = local("hello")
    let t: str = other(&mut x, &mut y, 42u16)
    ⎕empty(y)
    let u: str = kept(t)
    if s ≠ "hello<-" ∨ t ≠ "42" ∨ u ≠ "42" ∨ literal() ≠ "lit":
        return 1u6
    0u6
